69
00:05:17,240 --> 00:05:22,880
One of the big challenges with such high-capacity memory is that the probability of a bit flip

70
00:05:22,880 --> 00:05:25,960
or bit failure becomes significant.

71
00:05:25,960 --> 00:05:30,040
Bit flips are considered soft errors in that they do not introduce permanent failures to

72
00:05:30,040 --> 00:05:31,040
the device.

73
00:05:31,040 --> 00:05:36,080
Instead, they are transient and simply need to be reverted back to the correct state.

74
00:05:36,080 --> 00:05:41,480
These bit flips can occur due to gamma rays and alpha particle strikes or electrical interference,

75
00:05:41,480 --> 00:05:45,360
and the likelihood of errors is increasing with smaller feature sizes.

76
00:05:45,360 --> 00:05:51,640
So the standard solution is to employ ECC.

77
00:05:51,640 --> 00:05:56,720
For practical implementations of ECC, we leverage the fact that the probability of bit errors

78
00:05:56,720 --> 00:05:59,840
increases dramatically when we assume more errors.

79
00:05:59,840 --> 00:06:04,960
In other words, given a single memory word, the probability of one bit in it flipping

80
00:06:04,960 --> 00:06:09,360
is much greater than the probability of two bits in it flipping, which is much greater

81
00:06:09,360 --> 00:06:13,000
than more than two bits flipping.

82
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:17,360
This has limited practical ECC implementations to generally provide protection up to two

83
00:06:17,360 --> 00:06:18,360
errors.

84
00:06:18,360 --> 00:06:24,320
Now, there are two forms of protection, error detection and error correction.

85
00:06:24,320 --> 00:06:28,200
For error detection, we are only concerned with being able to recognize that some number

86
00:06:28,200 --> 00:06:33,120
of bits have flipped, in which case we'll signal a problem to the OS to deal with it

87
00:06:33,120 --> 00:06:38,520
or to kill the program to prevent the generation of incorrect results.

